f  of international foreign exchange  trading and it's about making sure that  we think about the innovation piece as  well as the risks and the concern i had  is that in the debate  understandably regulators are saying  well there's this risk and there's that  risk and they're  seriously problematic and of course  these things need to be looked at but  we've got to remember that being the  home to fintech is very strongly in  britain's national interest and it's  also a huge opportunity that we've got i  mean bitcoin which is obviously the best  known cryptocurrency is off by 46 since  early november i mean these are not  assets necessarily for widows and  orphans are they  well they're they're risk assets right  there are  there's different parts of of  cryptocurrencies the the stable coins  which are essentially part of the  payment system uh and then there's the  risk assets and yeah prices go up and  prices go down they go up and down in  equities as well you know you and i have  both been around long enough to have  seen the uh  uh the you know the the ups and downs of  equity markets over the years currencies  go uh up and down and  we we say caveat emptor we say that  you've got to you should invest what  your what you can  what you're prepared to lose  in high high risk assets so  that is about having a good regulatory  system so that people are aware of the  risks but let's let people take those  risks as well because it's only that way  that you're going to get the sort of  innovation that that can benefit us all  and that can make sure that london stays  ahead and you know with the freedoms  that we now have to to design our own  regulatory structures  we should be making sure that we can  attract new fintech of which uh uh you  know bitcoin and the other  cryptocurrencies are one example but  there's endless other examples of  innovations in this space and we should  be really embracing it that's that's  that's the case that i think needs to be  made to weigh against the understandable  concerns because otherwise you'd have an  imbalanced system  well look while we have you uh there's  obviously a photograph that's done the  rounds of what looks very much like a  party in the downing street garden where  you were attending what was going  through your mind when you were there  oh well  in that instance i mean i was in downing  street a lot during the pandemic  understandably  on the the day in question that you  talked about um i'd done the press  conference i went down to debrief the  prime minister  and then i left and all of that's for  the uh for the public record  and often there were meetings that were  held outdoors uh partly because it was  partly because it was safer um but what  we yeah what i was concentrating on was  obviously the huge amount of work that  we had to do during the pandemic and  that was at the forefront of my mind but  when other photographs emerged of you  apparently breaking lockdown rules you  resigned why is it you think that some  other members of the government have  seemingly got away with it  well i think you've got to look at the  big picture right it's been a tough  few years  but i think ultimately the government  has got the big calls right  if you look at the  for instance the vaccination program you  know we ended up with the  fastest vaccination program in europe  one of the fastest in the world not by  accident but because of actions taken  and decisions that we took  in order to make sure that we for  instance bought a head and bought a  basket of  uh  of vaccines  so that if in the end two of them came  off in in in big numbers oxford and uh  uh and pfizer and moderna to a slightly  smaller scale but still important  and we bought six  in the end we bought seven so that and  some of those didn't come off that's  actually about taking you know  knowing that not everything will come  off but going broad and investing across  a whole range of different options and  then in in our case two of them came off  and because of a very very innovative  uh health regulatory regime thanks to  the mhra you know we managed to get that  to  um to into people's arms as quickly as  possible so actually the there is a lot  of lessons from that in how we need to  regulate in the future as we can flex  our regulations post-brexit uh because  we managed in that case to say the  regulation of new vaccines  the phases should be done in  parallel instead of in series that  radically sped up the approvals process  the mhra used modern data techniques to  understand  the risks and to check that the vaccines  were safe and we need to make sure that  in a you know in a different sphere in  this case in health regulation that  that's dynamic as well and pro  innovation so actually you know i came  on to talk about cryptocurrency and  that's but it's actually a lesson that  we should learn from the pandemic right  across the board that you can improve  regulatory systems to get things quicker  to market or in the case of the vaccines  into the you know the arms of the  population okay whilst also protecting  against the risks right that's that's uh  all good and fun you've made that point  in the meantime of course your  colleagues are sending in letters of no  confidence to the 1922 committee  questioning the integrity of the prime  minister i mean do you still think he  has the moral authority to lead the  country and lead the party  yes because i think it comes back to  this big question about getting the big  calls right  and the vaccines is one example there's  other examples as well you know we're  one of the most open economies in the  world and for all the economic  challenges that we have and we have some  serious economic challenges and  obviously the the bank of england's  looking at the inflation data and we'll  hear from them later today  for all those challenges we do have the  fastest growing economy in the g7 and we  have one of the most open societies of  all major countries i mean it's easy to  forget that because we're sort of  getting back to normal and that here  um at least the  the the disease covered 19 is now  endemic not  pandemic that is a good thing but it  isn't true in many many other similar  countries across the world so look at  the big picture calls the vaccines the  consequence of the vaccines which is  that we're we're we're more open than  almost any comparator country uh and  you've got to you've got to look at the  big picture  sure the other big picture though is  that the prime minister's poll ratings  are in free fall i mean is this going to  do lasting damage to the conservative  party do you think  well i hope not and i think not i think  that what we have to concentrate on  is the future is on making sure that we  address the challenges ahead of us so  for instance making sure we get through  the backlog in the nhs but the on my  argument and one of the reasons that we  talked about you know me coming on today  is we've got to be pro innovation we've  got to be pro enterprise we've got to  make that argument more strongly than we  have because the only way out of the big  picture problems that we face  is through  enterprise and growth and making sure  that we get the investments here in the  uk we have a big opportunity to make  that happen so whether it's on  cryptocurrency or on uh  healthcare both of which we've talked  about right across the board we've got  to make sure we are the country that is  open to enterprise and we have a great  environment for enterprise and we have  the investment coming in here and that  is what worries me most frankly making  sure  that that as we come out of the pandemic  we can say to people who want to come  and take a risk come and take a risk  here in the uk well it worries a lot of  people tony danke from the cbi told us  this morning that uh the government's  condemning us to be high taxes and low  growth  well i actually i read his full article  and uh because i saw the headline and i  thought that's a bit of a surprise  coming from the cbi so i i read the full  article and actually it was remarkably  similar which is we've got to make sure  that we support enterprise we support  innovative fast-growing companies we  make it easy to make investments you  know most  most international companies  we need to make sure that the u their uk  arm is winning their argument at global  board level that the next investment  should be in the uk you know this is  where we need to be because it's how we  get ourselves out of the  you know the very significant economic  challenges that we like every country  around the world face

